<article class="env-example" id="064C">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/064C">Example <span data-tag="064C">9.2.5.3</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be a category which admits small filtered colimits. Then the nerve $\operatorname{N}_{\bullet }(\operatorname{\mathcal{C}})$ is an $\infty $-category which admits small filtered colimits (Corollary <a data-tag="039G" href="/tag/039G">9.2.1.13</a>). Moreover, an object $C \in \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is compact (in the sense of Definition <a data-tag="063B" href="/tag/063B">9.2.0.2</a>) if and only if it is compact when viewed as an object of the $\infty $-category $\operatorname{N}_{\bullet }(\operatorname{\mathcal{C}})$ (in the sense of Definition <a data-tag="064A" href="/tag/064A">9.2.5.1</a>). This follows from Proposition <a data-tag="06DZ" href="/tag/06DZ">9.2.2.25</a>, since the inclusion functor $\operatorname{N}_{\bullet }(\operatorname{Set}) \hookrightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{S}}$ is finitary (see Variant <a data-tag="03DH" href="/tag/03DH">9.1.6.4</a>). </p>
